reactions","Chemical calculations","Solutions and acids","Science inquiry"],"subtopics":["Ionic metallic and covalent bonding","Formulae nomenclature and equations","Moles mass and composition","Solutions solubility and concentration","Experimental design data and measurement"],"mixed":true,"text":"(8 marks) The solubility curves below show the change in solubility of a number of ionic compounds in water with a rise in temperature. Use the graph to answer the following questions. a. What can you infer about the relationship between temperature and solubility of ionic compounds in water? (1) b. Which of the compounds shown is the most soluble at 10.0°C? (1) c. Which compound is the most soluble at 40.0°C? (1) d. Estimate the extra mass of copper sulfate that could be dissolved in 100 mL water at 80.0°C, if 15g of copper sulfate has already been dissolved. (2) e. Would an aqueous solution of 45g potassium nitrate in 100 mL water be saturated at 40.0C? Justify your answer. (3)","html":"<div><p><strong>(8 marks)</strong></p>\n<p>The solubility curves below show the change in solubility of a number\nof ionic compounds in water with a rise in temperature.</p>\n<p>Use the graph to answer the following questions.</p>\n<figure><img src=\"assets/chemistry/practice-import-additional/0e7838ecd48d799983486ad4.webp\" alt=\"Original solubility graph\" data-word-width-px=\"500\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\"></figure><p>a. What can you infer about the relationship between temperature and\nsolubility of ionic compounds in water? (1)</p>\n<p>b. Which of the compounds shown is the most soluble at 10.0°C?  (1)</p>\n<p>c. Which compound is the most soluble at 40.0°C?  (1)</p>\n<p>d. Estimate the extra mass of copper sulfate that could be dissolved\nin 100 mL water at 80.0°C, if 15g of copper sulfate has already been\ndissolved. (2)</p>\n<p>e. Would an aqueous solution of 45g potassium nitrate in 100 mL water\nbe saturated at 40.0C? Justify your answer. (3)</p>\n</div>","answerHtml":"<div><p><strong>Question 1 (8 marks)</strong></p>\n<p>The solubility curves below show the change in solubility of a number\nof ionic compounds in water with a rise in temperature.</p>\n<p>Use the graph to answer the following questions.</p>\n<figure><img src=\"assets/chemistry/practice-import-additional/0e7838ecd48d799983486ad4.webp\" alt=\"Original solubility graph\" data-word-width-px=\"500\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\"></figure><p>a. What can you infer about the relationship between temperature and\nsolubility of ionic compounds in water? (1)</p>\n<p><strong>The solubility of compounds increases with\ntemperature.</strong></p>\n<p>b. Which of the compounds shown is the most soluble at 10.0°C?\n<strong>…NaCl..</strong> (1)</p>\n<p>c. Which compound is the most soluble at 40.0°C?\n<strong>KNO3 </strong> (1)</p>\n<p>d. Estimate the extra mass of copper sulfate that could be dissolved\nin 100 mL water at 80.0°C, if 15g of copper sulfate has already been\ndissolved. (2)</p>\n<p><strong>At 80<sup>0</sup>C approximately 32-33g of copper sulfate is\nsoluble in 100 mL of water. This would mean that an extra 17-18g of\ncopper sulphate could be dissolved in the solution.</strong></p>\n<p>e. Would an aqueous solution of 45g potassium nitrate in 100 mL water\nbe saturated at 40.0C? Justify your answer. (3)</p>\n<p><strong>No. (1)</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>The graph shows that 50g of potassium nitrate is soluble in\n100 mL of water at 40C. (1)</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>An extra 5g of potassium nitrate can be added before the\nsolution is</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>saturated. (1)</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n</div>","options":[],"optionsHtml":[],"correctAnswer":null,"markingMode":"manual","solutionOrigin":"supplied","sourceFile":"2015 Test 4 Solutions solubility.docx","sourcePath":"resources/ATAR/Chemistry/Assessments/11/John XXIII/2015/test 4/2015 Test 4 Solutions solubility.docx","sourceBlobSHA":"6eb3fb86e7e4d7d822ee07bd49e66b8b321b5e75","sourceSHA256":"1380de1a24df167de2545d10a73bbd79cf34a38c9ac99ff19f4343dd4f727e18","sourceKeyFile":"2015 Test 4 Solutions MS.docx","sourceKeyPath":"resources/ATAR/Chemistry/Assessments/11/John XXIII/2015/test 4/2015 Test 4 Solutions MS.docx","sourceKeyBlobSHA":"cb9ec3ba0321523ea7b8d3f155c20b8a855921ed","sourceKeySHA256":"a07a9ff8c94a95af03c09fa97d9980a984b6f5c4450af73c8eba2deed81461b4","sourceKeyNumber":1,"bankFile":"practice-studio-chemistry-john-solutions2015.json","addedAt":"2026-11-07T00:21:00.500397Z","editableText":true,"questionFormat":"native-text","fontFamily":"Arial","fontSizePt":12,"responseLines":0,"sourceReviewNotes":[],"sourceTextFile":"2015 Test 4 Solutions solubility.docx","sourceQuestionOccurrence":1,"types":["Short answer"],"figures":1,"hash":"7af526275c6a3812cb8f4220ed6cc2ef59b391c0461502c635814b24deaeddcc"},{"id":"chemistry-additional-john-solutions2015-written-2","subject":"Chemistry","year":11,"examYear":2015,"units":"1 & 2","number":2,"source":"Solutions and Solubility (John XXIII, 2015)","section":"Short answer","marks":6,"topics":["Atomic structure and periodicity"],"subtopics":["Electron configuration and periodic trends"],"mixed":false,"text":"(6 marks) Using sodium chloride and hydrogen chloride as examples, explain the similarities and differences between ‘ionisation’ and ‘dissociation’.","html":"<div><p><strong>(6 marks)</strong></p>\n<p>Using sodium chloride and hydrogen chloride as examples, explain the\nsimilarities and differences between ‘ionisation’ and\n‘dissociation’.</p>\n</div>","answerHtml":"<div><p><strong>Question 2 (6 marks)</strong></p>\n<p>Using sodium chloride and hydrogen chloride as examples, explain the\nsimilarities and differences between ‘ionisation’ and ‘dissociation’.\nYou must provide equations in your answer.</p>\n<p><strong>Similarity: both substances produce ions (1) in solution\nand</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>are strong electrolytes (1)</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Differences:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>NaCl is an ionic substance (1) and will dissociate into its\nions in solution (1)</strong></p>\n<p><strong>HCl is covalent, but a strong acid (1), will completely\nionise (1)</strong></p>\n</div>","options":[],"optionsHtml":[],"correctAnswer":null,"markingMode":"manual","solutionOrigin":"supplied","sourceFile":"2015 Test 4 Solutions solubility.docx","sourcePath":"resources/ATAR/Chemistry/Assessments/11/John XXIII/2015/test 4/2015 Test 4 Solutions solubility.docx","sourceBlobSHA":"6eb3fb86e7e4d7d822ee07bd49e66b8b321b5e75","sourceSHA256":"1380de1a24df167de2545d10a73bbd79cf34a38c9ac99ff19f4343dd4f727e18","sourceKeyFile":"2015 Test 4 Solutions MS.docx","sourceKeyPath":"resources/ATAR/Chemistry/Assessments/11/John XXIII/2015/test 4/2015 Test 4 Solutions MS.docx","sourceKeyBlobSHA":"cb9ec3ba0321523ea7b8d3f155c20b8a855921ed","sourceKeySHA256":"a07a9ff8c94a95af03c09fa97d9980a984b6f5c4450af73c8eba2deed81461b4","sourceKeyNumber":2,"bankFile":"practice-studio-chemistry-john-solutions2015.json","addedAt":"2026-11-07T00:21:00.500397Z","editableText":true,"questionFormat":"native-text","fontFamily":"Arial","fontSizePt":12,"responseLines":0,"sourceReviewNotes":[],"sourceTextFile":"2015 Test 4 Solutions solubility.docx","sourceQuestionOccurrence":1,"types":["Short answer"],"figures":0,"hash":"166a92992f542d192f9e763aaa3ca973d5290f67833cf81b0014e016a924ad3e"},{"id":"chemistry-additional-john-solutions2015-written-3","subject":"Chemistry","year":11,"examYear":2015,"units":"1 & 2","number":3,"source":"Solutions and Solubility (John XXIII, 2015)","section":"Short answer","marks":8,"topics":["Chemical calculations","Solutions and acids"],"subtopics":["Moles mass and composition","Solutions solubility and concentration"],"mixed":true,"text":"(8 marks) For the following determine: a. The concentration of the resulting solution when 60.50 grams of CuSO4 is dissolved in 250mL of water. (3) b. The final concentration when 25.0 mL of 12 mol L-1 HCL is diluted with water to give a final volume of 100.0 mL. (2) c. The mass of potassium carbonate required to prepare 150.0 mL of 0.250 mol L-1 solution. (3)","html":"<div><p><strong>(8 marks)</strong></p>\n<p>For the following determine:</p>\n<p>a. The concentration of the resulting solution when 60.50 grams of\nCuSO<sub>4</sub> is dissolved in 250mL of water. (3)</p>\n<p>b. The final concentration when 25.0 mL of 12 mol L<sup>-1</sup> HCL\nis diluted with water</p>\n<p>to give a final volume of 100.0 mL. (2)</p>\n<p>c. The mass of potassium carbonate required to prepare 150.0 mL\nof</p>\n<p>0.250 mol L<sup>-1</sup> solution. (3)</p>\n</div>","answerHtml":"<div><p><strong>Question 3 (8 marks)</strong></p>\n<p>For the following determine:</p>\n<p>a. The concentration of the resulting solution when 60.50 grams of\nCuSO<sub>4</sub> is dissolved in 250mL of water. (3)</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>n (CuSO4) = 60.5/159.62 = 0.37903 mol (1)</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>c = n/V = 0.37903/0.25 = 1.5 mol L<sup>-1</sup>\n(1)</strong></p>\n<p><strong>mol L<sup>-1</sup> (1) must have correct units for extra\nmark</strong></p>\n<p>b. The final concentration when 25.0mL of 12 mol L<sup>-1</sup> HCL\nis diluted with water</p>\n<p>to give a final volume of 100.0 mL. (2)</p>\n<p><strong>C1 x V1 = C2 x V2</strong></p>\n<p><strong>C2 = C1 x V1/V2 = 12 x 25/100 = 3.0 (1)</strong></p>\n<p><strong>mol/L (1) must have correct units for extra mark</strong></p>\n<p>c. The mass of potassium carbonate required to prepare 150.0 ml\nof</p>\n<p>0.250 mol L<sup>-1</sup> solution. (3)</p>\n<p><strong>C = n/V therefore n = c x V 0.25 mol/L x 0.15 L\n(1)</strong></p>\n<p><strong>nK2CO3 = 0.0375 mol (1)</strong></p>\n<p><strong>mK2CO3 = n x M = 0.0375 x 138.21 = 5.18 g (1)</strong></p>\n<p><strong>must have correct units for full marks</strong></p>\n</div>","options":[],"optionsHtml":[],"correctAnswer":null,"markingMode":"manual","solutionOrigin":"supplied","sourceFile":"2015 Test 4 Solutions solubility.docx","sourcePath":"resources/ATAR/Chemistry/Assessments/11/John XXIII/2015/test 4/2015 Test 4 Solutions solubility.docx","sourceBlobSHA":"6eb3fb86e7e4d7d822ee07bd49e66b8b321b5e75","sourceSHA256":"1380de1a24df167de2545d10a73bbd79cf34a38c9ac99ff19f4343dd4f727e18","sourceKeyFile":"2015 Test 4 Solutions MS.docx","sourceKeyPath":"resources/ATAR/Chemistry/Assessments/11/John XXIII/2015/test 4/2015 Test 4 Solutions MS.docx","sourceKeyBlobSHA":"cb9ec3ba0321523ea7b8d3f155c20b8a855921ed","sourceKeySHA256":"a07a9ff8c94a95af03c09fa97d9980a984b6f5c4450af73c8eba2deed81461b4","sourceKeyNumber":3,"bankFile":"practice-studio-chemistry-john-solutions2015.json","addedAt":"2026-11-07T00:21:00.500397Z","editableText":true,"questionFormat":"native-text","fontFamily":"Arial","fontSizePt":12,"responseLines":0,"sourceReviewNotes":[],"sourceTextFile":"2015 Test 4 Solutions solubility.docx","sourceQuestionOccurrence":1,"types":["Short answer"],"figures":0,"hash":"9ae6ad86a11e21c085cfa9f253d5d2e36f5fa5d71ef2f8db35cbab904eaf0a37"},{"id":"chemistry-additional-john-solutions2015-written-4","subject":"Chemistry","year":11,"examYear":2015,"units":"1 & 2","number":4,"source":"Solutions and Solubility (John XXIII, 2015)","section":"Short answer","marks":4,"topics":["Chemical bonding and structure","Solutions and acids"],"subtopics":["Allotropes nanomaterials and material properties","Solutions solubility and concentration","Acids bases and indicators"],"mixed":true,"text":"(4 marks) A student tested the conductivity of solutions of: a. sodium hydroxide b. acetic acid c. sugar. Draw a labelled diagram of the apparatus you would use to test the conductivity of the solutions.","html":"<div><p><strong>(4 marks)</strong></p>\n<p>A student tested the conductivity of solutions of:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>a. sodium hydroxide</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p>b. acetic acid</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p>c. sugar.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Draw a labelled diagram of the apparatus you would use to test the\nconductivity of the solutions.</p>\n</div>","answerHtml":"<div><p><strong>Question 4 (4 marks)</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1 mark for powerpack/source of electricity</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1 mark for electrodes</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1 mark for electrolyte</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1 mark for light bulb/ammeter</strong></p>\n</div>","options":[],"optionsHtml":[],"correctAnswer":null,"markingMode":"manual","solutionOrigin":"supplied","sourceFile":"2015 Test 4 Solutions solubility.docx","sourcePath":"resources/ATAR/Chemistry/Assessments/11/John XXIII/2015/test 4/2015 Test 4 Solutions solubility.docx","sourceBlobSHA":"6eb3fb86e7e4d7d822ee07bd49e66b8b321b5e75","sourceSHA256":"1380de1a24df167de2545d10a73bbd79cf34a38c9ac99ff19f4343dd4f727e18","sourceKeyFile":"2015 Test 4 Solutions MS.docx","sourceKeyPath":"resources/ATAR/Chemistry/Assessments/11/John XXIII/2015/test 4/2015 Test 4 Solutions MS.docx","sourceKeyBlobSHA":"cb9ec3ba0321523ea7b8d3f155c20b8a855921ed","sourceKeySHA256":"a07a9ff8c94a95af03c09fa97d9980a984b6f5c4450af73c8eba2deed81461b4","sourceKeyNumber":4,"bankFile":"practice-studio-chemistry-john-solutions2015.json","addedAt":"2026-11-07T00:21:00.500397Z","editableText":true,"questionFormat":"native-text","fontFamily":"Arial","fontSizePt":12,"responseLines":0,"sourceReviewNotes":[],"sourceTextFile":"2015 Test 4 Solutions solubility.docx","sourceQuestionOccurrence":1,"types":["Short answer"],"figures":0,"hash":"af4e51141142fdc9012c6da23bf819f3cb8f586aeb8b997e0b2e4bd444b2b5e1"},{"id":"chemistry-additional-john-solutions2015-written-5","subject":"Chemistry","year":11,"examYear":2015,"units":"1 & 2","number":5,"source":"Solutions and Solubility (John XXIII, 2015)","section":"Short answer","marks":4,"topics":["Chemical calculations","Solutions and acids"],"subtopics":["Moles mass and composition","Solutions solubility and concentration"],"mixed":true,"text":"(4 marks) 0.200 moles of solid MgCl2 are added to a 1.20 L of 1.40 mol L–1 NaCl solution. Calculate the concentration, in mol L–1, of Cl– ions in the new solution after both the solutions are combined.","html":"<div><p><strong>(4 marks)</strong></p>\n<p>0.200 moles of solid MgCl<sub>2</sub> are added to a 1.20 L of 1.40\nmol L<sup>–1</sup> NaCl solution.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Calculate the concentration, in mol L<sup>–1</sup>, of Cl<sup>–</sup>\nions in the new solution after both the solutions are combined.</p>\n</blockquote>\n</div>","answerHtml":"<div><p><strong>Question 5 (4 marks)</strong></p>\n<p>0.200 moles of solid MgCl<sub>2</sub> are added to a 1.20 L of 1.40\nmol L<sup>–1</sup> NaCl solution.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Calculate the concentration, in mol L<sup>–1</sup>, of Cl<sup>–</sup>\nions in the ‘new’ solution after both the solutions are combined.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>n(Cl<sup>–</sup>) from MgCl<sub>2</sub> = 0.400 mol\n<strong>(1)</strong></p>\n<p><em>n</em>(Cl<sup>–</sup>) from NaCl = <em>c</em>V</p>\n<p>= 1.40 × 1.20</p>\n<p>= 1.680 mo l <strong>(1)</strong></p>\n<p>total amount of Cl<sup>–</sup> = 2.08 mol in a</p>\n<p>volume of 1.20 L <strong>(1)</strong></p>\n<p>concentration of Cl<em><sup>–</sup></em> =\n</p>\n<p>=</p>\n<p>= 1.73 mol L<sup>–1</sup> <strong>(1)</strong></p>\n</div>","options":[],"optionsHtml":[],"correctAnswer":null,"markingMode":"manual","solutionOrigin":"supplied","sourceFile":"2015 Test 4 Solutions solubility.docx","sourcePath":"resources/ATAR/Chemistry/Assessments/11/John XXIII/2015/test 4/2015 Test 4 Solutions solubility.docx","sourceBlobSHA":"6eb3fb86e7e4d7d822ee07bd49e66b8b321b5e75","sourceSHA256":"1380de1a24df167de2545d10a73bbd79cf34a38c9ac99ff19f4343dd4f727e18","sourceKeyFile":"2015 Test 4 Solutions MS.docx","sourceKeyPath":"resources/ATAR/Chemistry/Assessments/11/John XXIII/2015/test 4/2015 Test 4 Solutions MS.docx","sourceKeyBlobSHA":"cb9ec3ba0321523ea7b8d3f155c20b8a855921ed","sourceKeySHA256":"a07a9ff8c94a95af03c09fa97d9980a984b6f5c4450af73c8eba2deed81461b4","sourceKeyNumber":5,"bankFile":"practice-studio-chemistry-john-solutions2015.json","addedAt":"2026-11-07T00:21:00.500397Z","editableText":true,"questionFormat":"native-text","fontFamily":"Arial","fontSizePt":12,"responseLines":0,"sourceReviewNotes":[],"sourceTextFile":"2015 Test 4 Solutions solubility.docx","sourceQuestionOccurrence":1,"types":["Short answer"],"figures":0,"hash":"e68be43ef4b43262c94adb7627b8638c04121470e71d9f4471eb729323e88da1"}]